# Rank 1209 / CVE-2024-43441: Apache HugeGraph Server's JWT signing key
# defaults to a fixed literal shipped in source. AuthOptions.java
# (hugegraph-server/hugegraph-core/.../config/AuthOptions.java) declares the
# auth.token_secret ConfigOption with the literal
# "FXQXbJtbCLxODc6tGci732pkH1cyf8Qg" as its fourth constructor argument (the
# option's default value), and TokenGenerator (.../auth/TokenGenerator.java)
# reads that option straight into the HMAC-SHA256 key used to sign and verify
# every auth JWT (`Keys.hmacShaKeyFor(secretKey.getBytes(...))`,
# `.signWith(this.key, SignatureAlgorithm.HS256)`) whenever a deployment does
# not override auth.token_secret in its own config file. Anyone who reads the
# public source tree has the exact key every unconfigured HugeGraph server
# signs and verifies tokens with, and can forge an admin JWT (CWE-798,
# CWE-321, CWE-347). Fix 03b40a52446218c83e98cb43020e0593a744a246 (git show,
# AuthOptions.java and StandardAuthManager.java only) replaces the literal
# fourth argument with a call to a new generateRandomBase64Key() helper (a
# SecureRandom-backed 32-byte key, base64-encoded) and logs that the key is
# now generated at startup. No flag on this pool row; confirmed the commit is
# the real, targeted fix — not a release bump — by reading its diff directly:
# it touches exactly the two files above, matching CVE-2024-43441's
# description, and nothing else.
#
# ATTENTION, engine gap, with one real binding shipped alongside it. `vyql
# match`/`vyql scan` on both the vulnerable and the fixed form of
# AuthOptions.java label nothing at auth.token_secret's declaration either
# way — no source, sink, or issue. The reason is not that Java lacks the
# vocabulary: `code.HardcodedSecretLiteral` / VYQL-AUTH-101 already exists,
# and the language-neutral lowering pass that feeds it
# (secretNamedTarget + plausibleSecretLiteral in
# internal/extract/lowering/lowering.go, case nir.Assign) already runs for
# every language sharing nir.Assign — python and javascript already had a
# binding consuming it (`callee.analysis ~= "secret.hardcoded" -> emit issue
# code.HardcodedSecretLiteral`), java had none at all, confirmed by grepping
# every vyql/bindings/java/*.vyql file for HardcodedSecretLiteral before this
# change: zero matches. Added that binding here (hardcodedSecretLiteral460 in
# vyql/bindings/java/m420f6b67.vyql) — a straight port of the existing
# python/javascript binding, naming no CVE-specific API — and it does close a
# real Java coverage-parity gap: a secret-named field or local assigned a
# literal directly now fires VYQL-AUTH-101 in Java for the first time. The
# lowering pass tags this analysis call's evidence by which heuristic fired —
# "secret=" for secretNamedTarget's secret/jwt/hmac/api_key/… family,
# "credential_const=" for credentialConstName's separate PASSWORD/USERNAME
# family — and the binding is scoped to `args.any.literal contains "secret="`
# rather than accepting both: the first bounded scan of the real checkout
# with an unscoped binding reported VYQL-AUTH-101 on
# hugegraph-server/hugegraph-core's own HugeUser.java:241,
# `PASSWORD = "~user_password"` — a false positive, that field names a graph
# schema property key, not a credential value, and the same
# SCREAMING_CASE-constant-named-PASSWORD shape is common in ORM/graph schema
# code generally. Scoping to "secret=" only keeps the direct-assignment case
# this CVE's own vocabulary belongs to (jwt/api_key/signing-key family) while
# dropping the noisier credential-constant family; rescanning the same
# checkout with the scoped binding reports zero VYQL-AUTH-101 findings
# anywhere, HugeUser.java included. What the new binding does NOT close is
# this CVE's own shape: `plausibleSecretLiteral`'s value only ever comes from
# `constStr(st.Value)` — the literal directly on the right of `=` — and
# AuthOptions.java's right-hand side is `new ConfigOption<>(name, desc,
# checker, "FXQX...")`, a constructor CALL, not a literal, so
# `analysis.secret.hardcoded` is never emitted for it regardless of which
# binding consumes it.
#
# Confirmed the binding-language ceiling directly, not just inferred it:
# tried a binding using a positional argument predicate
# (`args[3].literal contains "x"`) to reach the fourth constructor argument
# directly, since `callee.path == "ConfigOption"` correctly matches the `new
# ConfigOption<>(...)` call (constructors are addressed by class name
# elsewhere in this file, e.g. unsafeYamlConstructor140's
# `node.path == "org.yaml.snakeyaml.constructor.Constructor"`). Building it
# in produced, verbatim: `vyql: frontend: invalid binding corpus for java:
# bindings/java/<file>: binding testArgsPositional: query predicate
# "args[3].literal" is not implemented in scanner IR lowering`. The only
# argument-literal predicate the `where`-clause compiler implements is
# `args.any.literal contains "..."` (existential across every argument,
# confirmed by grep: no `args[N].` predicate appears in any `where` clause
# across the entire vyql/bindings/ corpus, in any of the ~10 languages —
# every existing `args[N]` use is in an `emit ... at` location, never a
# predicate), and that cannot discriminate this CVE's two revisions: the
# option's name ("auth.token_secret") and description ("Secret key of HS256
# algorithm.") literals are byte-identical before and after the fix, so
# `args.any.literal contains "secret"` matches both, and the only argument
# that changes — the fourth, going from a literal to a
# `generateRandomBase64Key()` call — is exactly the one no implemented
# predicate can single out. Reaching this CVE's shape needs either
# positional argument-literal predicates in the binding compiler, or
# extending the language-neutral secret-literal heuristic in lowering.go to
# unwrap a secret-named target's constructor-call arguments the way it
# already unwraps an `os.getenv(..., "default")` fallback
# (`envDefaultConst`) — both Go changes, not attempted here per this loop's
# instruction not to do engine work.
#
# Scanning the real checkout (bounded, timeout 180, --max-ram 4GB, --cache
# off; java:1176 bash:54 groovy:4 config:106 textpattern:1299) with the scoped
# binding in place confirms the same absence at scale: 848 findings total,
# zero of them VYQL-AUTH-101 anywhere in the tree, none on AuthOptions.java
# near auth.token_secret and none on HugeUser.java either.
#
# Spec below holds four cases: both real AuthOptions.java revisions (reject
# VYQL-AUTH-101 on each, pinning today's true state after the new binding
# ships) plus a CVE-independent isolating pair naming no HugeGraph API —
# the identical literal assigned directly to a secret-named field (expect
# VYQL-AUTH-101, proving the new binding and the underlying heuristic work)
# against the same literal passed as a config-option builder's default
# argument (reject VYQL-AUTH-101, isolating the one remaining gap to
# "argument of a call", not "assignment target" in general).

test "rank1209: AuthOptions declares auth.token_secret with a fixed literal default"
  lang java
  file AuthOptions.java
  reject VYQL-AUTH-101
  code
  ```
  package org.apache.hugegraph.config;

  import java.util.function.Predicate;

  class ConfigOption<T> {
      ConfigOption(String name, String description, Predicate<T> checker, T defaultValue) {
      }
  }

  class OptionChecker {
      static <T> Predicate<T> disallowEmpty() {
          return null;
      }
  }

  public class AuthOptions {

      public static final ConfigOption<String> AUTH_TOKEN_SECRET =
              new ConfigOption<>(
                      "auth.token_secret",
                      "Secret key of HS256 algorithm.",
                      OptionChecker.disallowEmpty(),
                      "FXQXbJtbCLxODc6tGci732pkH1cyf8Qg"
              );
  }
  ```

test "rank1209: AuthOptions generates auth.token_secret's default at startup"
  lang java
  file AuthOptions_fixed.java
  reject VYQL-AUTH-101
  code
  ```
  package org.apache.hugegraph.config;

  import java.security.SecureRandom;
  import java.util.Base64;
  import java.util.function.Predicate;

  class ConfigOption<T> {
      ConfigOption(String name, String description, Predicate<T> checker, T defaultValue) {
      }
  }

  class OptionChecker {
      static <T> Predicate<T> disallowEmpty() {
          return null;
      }
  }

  public class AuthOptions {

      public static final ConfigOption<String> AUTH_TOKEN_SECRET =
              new ConfigOption<>(
                      "auth.token_secret",
                      "Secret key of HS256 algorithm.",
                      OptionChecker.disallowEmpty(),
                      generateRandomBase64Key()
              );

      private static String generateRandomBase64Key() {
          SecureRandom random = new SecureRandom();
          byte[] bytes = new byte[32];
          random.nextBytes(bytes);
          return Base64.getEncoder().encodeToString(bytes);
      }
  }
  ```

# The isolating pair. Same literal, same secret-named-target heuristic; only
# whether it is assigned directly or passed as a builder call's argument
# differs.

test "rank1209: a secret-named field assigned the literal directly is labelled by the new java binding"
  lang java
  file DirectSecret.java
  expect VYQL-AUTH-101
  code
  ```
  package x;

  public class DirectSecret {
      public static final String AUTH_SECRET_KEY = "FXQXbJtbCLxODc6tGci732pkH1cyf8Qg";
  }
  ```

test "rank1209: the identical literal passed as a config-option builder's default argument is invisible"
  lang java
  file BuilderSecret.java
  reject VYQL-AUTH-101
  code
  ```
  package x;

  import java.util.function.Predicate;

  class ConfigOption<T> {
      ConfigOption(String name, String description, Predicate<T> checker, T defaultValue) {
      }
  }

  public class BuilderSecret {
      public static final ConfigOption<String> AUTH_SECRET_KEY =
              new ConfigOption<>(
                      "auth.secret_key",
                      "Secret key of HS256 algorithm.",
                      null,
                      "FXQXbJtbCLxODc6tGci732pkH1cyf8Qg"
              );
  }
  ```
